@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-allow-hour-aligned-sub-day-periods-alongside-the-whole-day-rule
+type: decision-log
+title: "Allow hour-aligned sub-day periods alongside the whole-day rule"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Customers can now rent by the hour: `addEquipment` accepts an optional `hourlyPrice`, and a rental
+shorter than a day that starts and ends on the hour is priced per hour instead of per day. `parsePeriod`
+(dl-001) previously required every period, for `quote`/`book`/`availability` alike, to start and end at
+UTC midnight. That rule must still hold for day-or-longer rentals, but an hourly rental legitimately
+starts and ends at a non-midnight hour.
+
+## Decision
+
+Relax `parsePeriod`'s alignment check from "a multiple of `DAY_MS`" to "a multiple of `HOUR_MS`" for both
+`start` and `end`, which both midnight and any other on-the-hour instant satisfy. Then, only when the
+resulting duration is a day or more, additionally require both ends to be a multiple of `DAY_MS`
+(midnight UTC), re-imposing the original whole-day rule exactly where it still applies. Whether a
+duration qualifies for hourly pricing is decided afterwards, in `resolveQuote`, by comparing
+`durationMs` to `DAY_MS` and requiring the item to carry an `hourlyPrice`; an item without one throws
+`item <id> cannot be rented by the hour` for any sub-day request. `parsePeriod` continues to be the one
+shared validation path for `quote`, `book` and `availability` (dl-003), so the hour/day period shapes
+can't drift between them.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A rental of a day or more is still rejected unless both ends are UTC midnight, preserving dl-001's
+  guarantee unchanged for that case.
+- A rental shorter than a day is now accepted when both ends fall on the hour, and priced per hour via
+  the item's `hourlyPrice`; the existing 3+ day discount (dl-004) only ever applies on the day-pricing
+  branch, since a discountable period is by definition a day or more.
+- A sub-day request for an item without `hourlyPrice` is a caller error surfaced immediately, matching
+  how an unknown item or unknown kind is already handled.
+- `overlaps`/`bookedPeriods` are untouched: an hourly `Period` and a whole-day `Period` compare correctly
+  against each other since both are just `{ startMs, endMs }` millisecond ranges.
