@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+---
+id: task-004-let-customers-rent-by-the-hour
+type: task
+title: "Let customers rent by the hour"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Many visitors only want a board for an afternoon. Let customers rent by the hour: `addEquipment` now also
+accepts an `hourlyPrice`, what one hour of rental costs. A rental shorter than a day that starts and ends
+on the hour is priced at the item's hourly price for each hour. An item without an hourly price cannot be
+rented by the hour.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added optional `hourlyPrice?: number` to the `Equipment` interface in `src/index.ts`, with a doc
+  comment, and validated it in `addEquipment` the same way `dailyPrice` is validated (non-negative
+  integer cents) when provided; omitting it is valid and means the item cannot be rented by the hour.
+- Relaxed `parsePeriod`'s alignment check from "multiple of a day" to "multiple of an hour" for both
+  `start` and `end`, then re-imposed the original midnight-UTC requirement only when the resulting
+  duration is a day or more. See dl-005 for the full rationale; this keeps one shared validation path for
+  `quote`, `book` and `availability` (dl-003) instead of a parallel one for hourly periods.
+- `resolveQuote` now branches on duration: under `DAY_MS` it requires the item to carry `hourlyPrice`
+  (else throws `item <id> cannot be rented by the hour`) and prices `hours * hourlyPrice`; a day or more
+  keeps the existing day-based `priceFor` path (dl-004's 3+ day discount), since an hourly rental is by
+  definition shorter than the discount threshold.
+- Updated the `RentalRequest`/`AvailabilityQuery` doc comments on `start`/`end` to describe both the
+  midnight and on-the-hour period shapes.
+- Test-first: added failing tests for an hourly quote, an hourly booking, a sub-day request for an item
+  without `hourlyPrice`, a sub-day period not aligned to the hour, a 24-hour period not aligned to
+  midnight (still rejected), invalid `hourlyPrice` values on `addEquipment`, and an hourly booking
+  blocking an overlapping hourly availability query — confirmed each failed against the prior
+  implementation, then implemented the feature to turn them green.
+- `npm test`: 26/26 passing (19 pre-existing + 7 new).
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- None identified; the hour/day boundary is the existing `DAY_MS`/`HOUR_MS` constants, so no new tunable
+  was introduced.
